 is \\(AB\\).  The line \\(MN\\) joins the centres of the circles; therefore it is the perpendicular bisector of the common chord \\(AB\\).  Hence \\(AB\\perp MN\\) and \\(M,N\\) are symmetric with respect to the midpoint of \\(AB\\).  Since \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on \\(MN\\), we have \\(AB\\parallel CD\\).  Moreover, the distances \\(MC=MD\\) (both equal the radius of \\(\\Omega\\)) and \\(NC=ND\\) (both equal the radius of \\(\\Gamma\\)), so the non‑parallel sides \\(AC\\) and \\(BD\\) are equal; thus \\(ABCD\\) is an isosceles trapezoid.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Inversion about \\(P\\) with radius \\(PA\\)\n\nDenote by \\(\\mathcal I\\) the inversion with centre \\(P\\) and power \\(PA^{2}\\).  For any point \\(X\\neq P\\) let \\(X'=\\mathcal I(X)\\).\n\n#### 2.1  Images of the given circles\n\nBoth \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) pass through \\(A\\) and intersect the inversion circle at \\(A\\).  Since \\(A\\) lies on the inversion circle, the image of each circle is a line passing through the image of the other intersection point of the circle with the inversion circle.  The second intersection of \\(\\Omega\\) with the inversion circle is \\(C\\) (because \\(C\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(PC=PA\\) – the latter follows from \\(P\\) being the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)).  Hence \\(\\mathcal I(\\Omega)\\) is the line \\(AC\\).  Similarly, \\(\\mathcal I(\\Gamma)\\) is the line \\(AD\\).\n\n#### 2.2  Images of \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nBy definition \\(E\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and on the line \\(AP\\).  Since \\(AP\\) is invariant under the inversion (it passes through the centre), the image \\(E'=\\mathcal I(E)\\) lies on the image of \\(\\Omega\\), i.e. on the line \\(AC\\), and also on \\(AP\\).  As \\(E\\neq A\\), we have \\(E'\\neq A\\); thus \\(E'\\) is the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with the line \\(AC\\).  An analogous argument shows that \\(F'\\) lies on the line \\(AD\\) and on \\(AP\\); hence \\(F'\\) is the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(AD\\).\n\n#### 2.3  Image of the circle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\)\n\nThe points \\(B\\) and \\(A\\) are the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  Under \\(\\mathcal I\\) they are sent to \\(A\\) and \\(A\\) respectively (because \\(B\\) lies on both circles, its image lies on both lines \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\); the only common point of these lines is \\(A\\)).  Consequently the circle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) is transformed into the circle passing through the images of \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) and through \\(A\\); that is,\n\\[\n\\mathcal I\\bigl(\\omega_{BEF}\\bigr)=\\omega_{AE'F'} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Image of the line \\(\\ell\\)\n\nRecall that \\(\\ell\\) is the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  Since \\(\\ell\\) does not contain the centre \\(P\\) of the inversion, its image \\(\\mathcal I(\\ell)\\) is a circle passing through \\(P\\).\n\nLet \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) across the line \\(MN\\).  In any triangle the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle of the triangle; therefore \\(H'\\in (PMN)\\).\n\nBecause of §1.1 we have \\(AP\\perp MN\\).  The quadrilateral \\(PH'MN\\) thus has opposite sides parallel (\\(PH'\\parallel MN\\) and \\(PM\\parallel H'N\\)), and adjacent sides perpendicular; consequently it is a rectangle.  Hence \\(PH'=PH\\) and the line \\(PH'\\) is the image of the line \\(\\ell\\) under the inversion (the inversion sends a line not through the centre to the circle through the centre whose diameter is the segment joining the centre with the image of any point on the line).  Therefore\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(\\ell)=\\bigl(PA H'\\bigr),\n\\]\nthe circle through \\(P\\), \\(A\\) and \\(H'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Tangency of \\((PAH')\\) and \\((AE'F')\\) at \\(A\\)\n\nTo prove that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) it suffices, by the properties of inversion, to show that the circles \\((PAH')\\) and \\((AE'F')\\) are tangent at \\(A\\).\n\nTwo circles meeting at a point are tangent there iff the angles formed by the two chords through that point are equal.  Hence we must verify\n\\[\n\\angle PAH'=\\angle AE'F'. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n#### 4.1  Evaluation of \\(\\angle PAH'\\)\n\nPoints \\(P,A,H',M,N\\) all lie on the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\).  In that circle the chord \\(PH'\\) subtends the same angle at \\(A\\) as it does at \\(M\\) (or at \\(N\\)).  Consequently\n\\[\n\\angle PAH'=\\angle PMN. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n#### 4.2  Evaluation of \\(\\angle AE'F'\\)\n\nFrom §2.2 we have \\(E',F'\\in AP\\) and \\(E'\\in AC,\\;F'\\in AD\\).  Because \\(P\\) is the centre of the circle \\((AE'F'C)\\) (all four points are at distance \\(PA\\) from \\(P\\)), the quadrilateral \\(AE'F'C\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\).  Hence the angle subtended by chord \\(AF'\\) at \\(E'\\) equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the centre:\n\\[\n\\angle AE'F'=\\angle APC. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n#### 4.3  Equality \\(\\angle PMN=\\angle APC\\)\n\nConsider the isosceles trapezoid \\(ABCD\\) from §1.2.  Its diagonals \\(AC\\) and \\(BD\\) intersect at a point \\(X\\) that lies on the line \\(MN\\) (the line of symmetry).  The circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(AD\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); consequently \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) and also on the line of symmetry \\(MN\\).  Therefore the chord \\(AC\\) sees the same angle from any point on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) as it does from the centre \\(P\\) of the circle \\((APC)\\).  More concretely, the points \\(M,N,P\\) are collinear with the midpoint of arc \\(AC\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); thus the arcs \\(AP\\) and \\(PC\\) are equal, giving\n\\[\n\\angle PMN = \\angle APC. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nA purely angle‑chasing verification: because \\(AB\\parallel CD\\), the exterior angle at \\(C\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) equals \\(\\angle ABC\\).  But \\(\\angle ABC\\) is the angle subtended by chord \\(AC\\) at point \\(B\\) on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  Since \\(P\\) is the midpoint of arc \\(AC\\) of that circumcircle, the central angle \\(\\angle APC\\) is twice \\(\\angle ABC\\).  On the other hand, in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the points \\(M,N\\) are symmetric with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\); consequently \\(\\angle PMN\\) also equals twice \\(\\angle ABC\\).  Hence \\(\\angle PMN=\\angle APC\\).\n\nCombining (2), (3) and (4) we obtain\n\\[\n\\angle PAH' = \\angle PMN = \\angle APC = \\angle AE'F',\n\\]\nwhich is precisely (1).  Therefore the circles \\((PAH')\\) and \\((AE'F')\\) are tangent at \\(A\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Returning to the original configuration\n\nSince inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) maps \\(\\ell\\) to the circle \\((PAH')\\) and maps \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) to \\(\\omega_{AE'F'}\\), the tangency of these two circles at \\(A\\) implies that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at the inverse of \\(A\\).  The inverse of \\(A\\) under \\(\\mathcal I\\) is the second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\); consequently \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at that point.\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\) was constructed to be parallel to \\(AP\\), we have proved the required statement:\n\n> **The line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).** ∎"
