N\\).  \nConsequently (2) gives\n\n\\[\nAP\\perp MN.\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n#### 1.2  \\(ABCD\\) is an isosceles trapezoid  \n\nThe line joining the centres of two intersecting circles is the perpendicular bisector of their common chord.  \nThus \\(MN\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\); in particular \\(AB\\perp MN\\).  \nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on \\(MN\\), we have \\(AB\\parallel CD\\).  \n\nMoreover,\n\\[\nMC=MD=r,\\qquad NC=ND=R,\n\\]\nso the non‑parallel sides \\(AC\\) and \\(BD\\) are equal (they are symmetric with respect to the axis \\(MN\\)).  \nHence \\(ABCD\\) is an isosceles trapezoid with bases \\(AB\\) and \\(CD\\) and axis of symmetry \\(MN\\).  \n\nFrom the symmetry we also obtain that the midpoint of \\(AB\\) lies on \\(MN\\); denote this midpoint by \\(O\\).  \n\n### 2.  Inversion about \\(P\\)  \n\nConsider the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(P\\) and power \\(k=PA^{2}\\).  \nFor any point \\(X\\neq P\\) write \\(X'=\\mathcal I(X)\\).\n\n#### 2.1  Images of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)\n\nBecause of (1), the points \\(A\\) and \\(C\\) lie on the inversion circle (the circle of radius \\(PA\\) centred at \\(P\\)).  \nA circle passing through the centre of inversion is mapped to a line; a circle passing through two points of the inversion circle is mapped to the line joining the images of the other points.  \nSince \\(\\Omega\\) passes through \\(A\\) and \\(C\\), its image is the line \\(A C\\).  \nSimilarly, \\(\\Gamma\\) passes through \\(A\\) and \\(D\\); its image is the line \\(A D\\).\n\nThus\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(\\Omega)=AC,\\qquad \\mathcal I(\\Gamma)=AD.\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n#### 2.2  Images of \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nBy construction, \\(E\\) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\).  \nThe line \\(AP\\) passes through the centre of inversion, so it is invariant under \\(\\mathcal I\\).  \nConsequently \\(E'\\) lies on both \\(AP\\) and the image of \\(\\Omega\\), i.e. on \\(AP\\cap AC\\).  \nSince