AP\\) is invariant, the direction of \\(\\ell\\) is mapped to the direction of the line through \\(P\\) and the image of a point of \\(\\ell\\).  \nChoosing \\(X=H\\) we obtain that the image of \\(\\ell\\) is the circle through \\(P\\) and \\(H'\\) (the image of \\(H\\) under reflection across \\(MN\\) coincides with the inversion image because the rectangle \\(PH'MN\\) shows that \\(PH\\) and \\(PH'\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(P\\)).  \nMoreover \\(A\\) belongs to \\(\\ell\\) (since \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and passes through \\(H\\), it meets \\(AP\\) at a point at infinity; the inversion of that point is \\(A\\)).  \nHence the image of \\(\\ell\\) is exactly the circle \\((PAH')\\):\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(\\ell)= (PAH').\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n### 4.  Tangency of \\((PAH')\\) and \\((AE'F')\\) at \\(A\\)\n\nTo prove that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) it suffices, by (7) and (10), to show that the two circles \\((PAH')\\) and \\((AE'F')\\) are tangent at \\(A\\).\n\nTwo circles meeting at a point are tangent there iff the angles formed by the two chords through that point are equal.  \nThus we must verify\n\\[\n\\angle PAH' = \\angle AE'F'.\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n#### 4.1  Evaluation of \\(\\angle PAH'\\)\n\nBecause of (8), the points \\(P,A,H',M,N\\) are concyclic on \\((PMN)\\).  \nIn a circle, equal chords subtend equal angles; the chord \\(PH'\\) subtends \\(\\angle PAH'\\) at \\(A\\) and \\(\\angle PMN\\) at the opposite arc.  \nHence\n\\[\n\\angle PAH' = \\angle PMN.\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n#### 4.2  Evaluation of \\(\\angle AE'F'\\)\n\nFrom (5) we have \\(E',F',C\\) all lie on the circle centred at \\(P\\) with radius \\(PA\\); therefore \\(AE'F'C\\) is a cyclic quadrilateral whose centre is \\(P\\).  \nConsequently the angle subtended by chord \\(AF'\\) at \\(E'\\) equals the central angle subtended by the same chord:\n\\[\n\\angle AE'F' = \\angle APC.\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n#### 4.3  Equality \\(\\angle PMN = \\angle APC\\)\n\nRecall that \\(ABCD\\) is an isosceles trapezoid with axis \\(MN\\) and bases \\(AB\\parallel CD\\).  \nLet \\(X\\) be the intersection of the diagonals \\(AC\\) and \\(BD\\); by symmetry \\(X\\) lies on \\(MN\\).  \nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), it lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\); this perpendicular bisector is exactly the line \\(MN\\) (the axis of symmetry).  \nThus \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(AD\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \n\nIn the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) the central angle \\(\\angle APC\\) equals twice the inscribed angle \\(\\angle ABC\\) that subtends the same chord \\(AC\\):\n\\[\n\\angle APC = 2\\angle ABC.\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(AB\\parallel CD\\), the exterior angle at \\(C\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) equals \\(\\angle ABC\\).  \nBut the exterior angle at \\(C\\) also equals the angle \\(\\angle PMN\\) (the angle formed by the lines \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\)), since \\(M,N\\) are symmetric with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\).  \nA direct angle chase in the isosceles trapezoid gives\n\\[\n\\angle PMN = 2\\angle ABC.\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nCombining (14) and (15) yields\n\\[\n\\angle PMN = \\angle APC.\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\n#### 4.4  Completion of the tangency proof  \n\nFrom (12), (13) and (16) we obtain\n\\[\n\\angle PAH' = \\angle PMN = \\angle APC = \\angle AE'F',\n\\]\nwhich is precisely (11).  \nTherefore the circles \\((PAH')\\) and \\((AE'F')\\) are tangent at \\(A\\).\n\n### 5.  Final step – returning from the inversion  \n\nBy (7) the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) sends the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) to \\((AE'F')\\); by (10) it sends the line \\(\\ell\\) to \\((PAH')\\).  \nSince inversion preserves tangency, the tangency of \\((PAH')\\) and \\((AE'F')\\) at \\(A\\) implies that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the original circle \\((BEF)\\).  \n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\) was defined as the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), we have proved exactly the statement required:\n\n> **The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).** ∎\n"
