[\n\\mathcal I(\\ell)= (PAH').\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n### 4.  Tangency of \\((PAH')\\) and \\((AE'F')\\) at \\(A\\)\n\nTo prove that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) it suffices, by (7) and (10), to show that the two circles \\((PAH')\\) and \\((AE'F')\\) are tangent at \\(A\\).\n\nTwo circles meeting at a point are tangent there iff the angles formed by the two chords through that point are equal.  \nThus we must verify\n\\[\n\\angle PAH' = \\angle AE'F'.\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n#### 4.1  Evaluation of \\(\\angle PAH'\\)\n\nBecause of (8), the points \\(P,A,H',M,N\\) are concyclic on \\((PMN)\\).  \nIn a circle, equal chords subtend equal angles; the chord \\(PH'\\) subtends \\(\\angle PAH'\\) at \\(A\\) and \\(\\angle PMN\\) at the opposite arc.  \nHence\n\\[\n\\angle PAH' = \\angle PMN.\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n#### 4.2  Evaluation of \\(\\angle AE'F'\\)\n\nFrom (5) we have \\(E',F',C\\) all lie on the circle centred at \\(P\\) with radius \\(PA\\); therefore \\(AE'F'C\\) is a cyclic quadrilateral whose centre is \\(P\\).  \nConsequently the angle subtended by chord \\(AF'\\) at \\(E'\\) equals the central angle subtended by the same chord:\n\\[\n\\angle AE'F' = \\angle APC.\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n#### 4.3  Equality \\(\\angle PMN = \\angle APC\\)\n\nRecall that \\(ABCD\\) is an isosceles trapezoid with axis \\(MN\\) and bases \\(AB\\parallel CD\\).  \nLet \\(X\\) be the intersection of the diagonals \\(AC\\) and \\(BD\\); by symmetry \\(X\\) lies on \\(MN\\).  \nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), it lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\); this perpendicular bisector is exactly the line \\(MN\\) (the axis of symmetry).  \nThus \\(P\\) is the midpoint of the arc \\(AD\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \n\nIn the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) the central angle \\(\\angle APC\\) equals twice the inscribed angle \\(\\angle ABC\\) that subtends the same chord \\(AC\\):\n\\[\n\\angle APC = 2\\angle ABC.\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(AB\\pa