parallel MN\\) and \\(PM\\parallel H'N\\)) and adjacent sides perpendicular, i.e. it is a rectangle.  \nHence  \n\n\\[\nPH'=PH\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\angle PH'M = 90^{\\circ}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2  Image of \\(\\ell\\) under the inversion  \n\nThe line \\(\\ell\\) does **not** pass through the centre \\(P\\) of the inversion, therefore its image is a circle passing through \\(P\\).  \nSince \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\) is invariant, the direction of \\(\\ell\\) is mapped to the direction of the line through \\(P\\) and the image of any point of \\(\\ell\\).  \nTaking the point \\(H\\in\\ell\\) and using (11) we see that the image of \\(H\\) is precisely \\(H'\\).  \nConsequently the image of the whole line \\(\\ell\\) is the circle determined by the three points \\(P\\), \\(A\\) (the point at infinity of the direction parallel to \\(AP\\) is sent to \\(A\\)) and \\(H'\\); that is,\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(\\ell)= (PAH').\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Tangency of \\((PAH')\\) and \\((AE'F')\\) at \\(A\\)\n\nTo prove that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) it suffices, by (9) and (12), to show that the two circles \\((PAH')\\) and \\((AE'F')\\) are tangent at \\(A\\).\n\nTwo circles meeting at a point are tangent there iff the angles formed by the two chords through that point are equal.  \nThus we have to verify\n\n\\[\n\\angle PAH' = \\angle AE'F'.\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1  Evaluation of \\(\\angle PAH'\\)\n\nBecause of (10) the points \\(P,A,H',M,N\\) are concyclic on \\((PMN)\\).  \nIn a circle equal chords subtend equal angles; the chord \\(PH'\\) subtends \\(\\angle PAH'\\) at \\(A\\) and \\(\\angle PMN\\) at the opposite arc. Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle PAH' = \\angle PMN .\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2  Evaluation of \\(\\angle AE'F'\\)\n\nFrom (6)–(7) the points \\(E',F',C\\) lie on the circle centred at \\(P\\) with radius \\(PA\\); consequently \\(AE'F'C\\) is a cyclic quadrilateral whose centre is \\(P\\).  \nTherefore the angle subtended by the chord \\(AF'\\) at \\(E'\\) equals the central angle subtended by the same chord:\n\n\\[\n\\angle AE'F' = \\angle APC .\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.3  Equality \\(\\angle PMN = \\angle APC\\)\n\nRecall that \\(AB\\) is the common chord of the two original circles; by the well‑known property of intersecting circles the line \\(MN\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\).  \nConsequently the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(AB\\) lies on \\(MN\\).  \n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segment \\(AP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); but \\(CD\\subset MN\\), so the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) coincides with the line \\(MN\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\nP\\in MN .\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nThus the points \\(M,N,P\\) are collinear.  \nFrom (2) we have \\(AP\\perp MN\\); therefore \\(AP\\) is also the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(MN\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\nPM = PN .\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nNow consider the isosceles triangle \\(PMN\\). Its vertex angle is \\(\\angle PMN\\); the base \\(MN\\) is seen from \\(P\\) under the same angle as the chord \\(CD\\) of the circle \\((ACD)\\). Because \\(P\\) is the centre of that circle,  \n\n\\[\n\\angle APC = 2\\angle ADC .\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(\\angle ADC\\) is an exterior angle of triangle \\(ACD\\); using the fact that \\(AB\\perp CD\\) (see (4)) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle ADC = \\angle PMN .\\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nCombining (18) and (19) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\angle APC = 2\\angle PMN .\\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nOn the other hand, in the isosceles triangle \\(PMN\\) the base angles are equal, and the sum of the three angles is \\(180^{\\circ}\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle PMN