ets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\); because \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\omega\\) intersect at the two points \\(A\\) and \\(C\\), the second intersection of the line \\(AE\\) with \\(\\omega\\) must be \\(C\\).  Similarly the second intersection of the line \\(AF\\) with \\(\\omega\\) is \\(D\\).  Consequently the directed angles satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CPA = \\angle CPD ,\\qquad \\angle BPA = \\angle BPE .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFrom (5) we see that the centre \\(P\\) is the centre of the **spiral similarity** that sends the segment \\(CD\\) to the segment \\(EF\\) and, because \\(AB\\) is the common chord of the two original circles, also sends \\(CD\\) to \\(AB\\).  In particular\n\n\\[\n\\angle APC = \\angle AEF = \\angle AFB .\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 3.  The orthocenter \\(H\\) and its reflection  \n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and let \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) in the line \\(MN\\).  \nIn any triangle the reflection of the orthocenter in a side lies on the circumcircle of the triangle; therefore  \n\n\\[\nH'\\in (PMN). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBecause of (2) the line \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\); hence the quadrilateral \\(PH'MN\\) has opposite sides parallel (\\(PH'\\parallel MN\\) and \\(PM\\parallel H'N\\)) and adjacent sides perpendicular, i.e. it is a rectangle.  From the rectangle we obtain  \n\n\\[\nPH'=PH ,\\qquad AH'\\parallel AP .\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is also parallel to \\(AH'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.  Tangency of two circles at \\(A\\)\n\nFrom (7) the points \\(P,M,N,H'\\) are concyclic; consequently the equal chords \\(PH'\\) and \\(PM\\) subtend equal angles at the circumference:\n\n\\[\n\\angle PAH' = \\angle PMN .\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBut the triangle \\(PMN\\) is isosceles because \\(PM=PN\\) (both equal the radius of \\(\\omega\\) by (4)).  Hen