y \\(\\mathcal S\\) to the reflection of \\(\\mathcal S(H)\\) in \\(AB\\).  The altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) is mapped by \\(\\mathcal S\\) onto the altitude from \\(A\\) to \\(AB\\); consequently the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is mapped onto the orthocentre of the degenerate triangle \\(A B B\\), i.e. onto the vertex \\(A\\).  Hence\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal S(H)=A . \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Angle chase and tangency.**  \n   Because \\(\\mathcal S\\) fixes the line \\(AP\\), the image under \\(\\mathcal S\\) of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), namely the line \\(AP\\) itself.  Using (1) and (2) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle\\bigl(\\,AP,\\; \\text{line through }H\\parallel AP\\,\\bigr)\n   =\\angle\\bigl(\\,AP,\\;AP\\,\\bigr)\n   =\\angle\\bigl(\\,BE,\\;BF\\,\\bigr) .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   The right‑hand side of (3) is precisely the angle between the chord \\(EF\\) of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) and its tangent at \\(E\\) (or at \\(F\\)).  Therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) makes with the chord \\(EF\\) the same angle as the tangent to \\((BEF)\\); consequently it is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\nThus the required tangency is proved.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of } \\triangle BEF.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The Miquel point of the quadrilateral \\(ABCD\\)\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) be the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the order on the line being \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\));  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nConsider the two circles \\((A B C)\\) and \\((A B D)\\).  They intersect at \\(A\\) and at a second point; denote this second intersection by \\(X\\).  The point \\(X\\) is the **Miquel point** of the complete quadrilateral formed by the four lines \\(AB,\\,CD,\\,MN\\) and the common chord \\(AB\\).\n\nBecause \\(X\\) lies on both circles \\((A B C)\\) and \\((A B D)\\), the directed angles satisfy\n\\[\n\\angle X C A = \\angle X B A ,\\qquad\n\\angle X D A = \\angle X B A .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\nHence the rotation–dilation (spiral similarity) \\(\\mathcal S\\) with centre \\(X\\) that sends the segment \\(CD\\) onto the segment \\(AB\\) also sends the line \\(AP\\) onto itself (both \\(A\\) and the image of \\(P\\) lie on the circle \\((A C D)\\), which is invariant under \\(\\mathcal S\\)).  In particular,\n\\[\n\\mathcal S(A)=A,\\qquad \\mathcal S(C)=B,\\qquad \\mathcal S(D)=B .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), applying \\(\\mathcal S\\) to the lines \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) yields\n\\[\n\\mathcal S(E)=B,\\qquad \\mathcal S(F)=B .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal S\\) maps the triangle \\(AEF\\) onto the degenerate triangle \\(A B B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nRecall the well‑known fact: *the reflection of the orthocentre of a triangle in any side lies on the circum‑circle of the triangle*.  Let \\(H'\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) in the side \\(MN\\); then\n\\[\nH'\\in (P M N). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) is the image of the line \\(AB\\) under the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) (by (2)