t the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\n### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete, rigorous proof.  \nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Identify the required line.**  \n   Because \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the chord \\(CD\\) is perpendicular to the radius \\(AP\\); hence \\(AP\\perp CD\\).  \n   The points \\(C,N,D\\) are collinear, so \\(CD\\subset MN\\).  \n   Consequently the altitude of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) issued from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AP\\); the orthocenter \\(H\\) lies on this altitude.  \n   Therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is exactly the line \\(HP\\).\n\n2. **Apply an inversion centred at \\(A\\).**  \n   Perform the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and arbitrary power \\(k^{2}>0\\).  \n   *Every circle through \\(A\\) is transformed into a line not passing through \\(A\\), and every line through \\(A\\) is transformed into a circle through \\(A\\).*  \n\n   Under \\(\\mathcal I\\) we have  \n\n   * \\(\\Omega\\) (which passes through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\)) is sent to a line \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) that passes through the image of the second intersection point \\(B\\); thus \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) is a line through \\(B\\).  \n   * \\(\\Gamma\\) is sent to another line \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) through the same point \\(B\\); in general \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\neq\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).  \n   * The points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) (the second intersections of the ray \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)) are sent to the points at infinity on \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) respectively.  \n   * Hence the circum‑circle \\((BEF)\\) is transformed into the line \\(\\ell\\) that passes through \\(B\\) and is parallel to both \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\); equivalently, \\(\\ell\\) is the **common perpendicular** to the two lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).  \n\n   Moreover, because \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have \\(PA=PC=PD\\); consequently the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are inverse to each other, and the circle \\((ACD)\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal I\\).  \n   The image of its centre \\(P\\) is a point \\(P'\\) that lies on the line \\(\\ell\\) (the image of the circle \\((BEF)\\)).  \n\n   The triangle \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is sent to \\(\\triangle P'MN\\).  \n   Since the centre of inversion \\(A\\) lies on the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) (the circle \\((ACD)\\) passes through \\(A\\) and has centre \\(P\\)), inversion **preserves orthocentres**: the image of the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the orthocentre \\(H'\\) of \\(\\triangle P'MN\\).\n\n3. **The inverted problem.**  \n   After inversion we have to prove that the line \\(HP\\) (which becomes the line \\(H'P'\\)) is tangent to the image of \\((BEF)\\), i.e. to the line \\(\\ell\\).  \n   Because \\(P'\\) lies on \\(\\ell\\), the tangency condition is equivalent to  \n\n   \\[\n   H'P'\\perp \\ell .\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n4. **A Simson‑line observation.**  \n   The line \\(\\ell\\) is the common perpendicular to \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\); by construction \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) are the images of the two circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n   Hence \\(\\ell\\) is precisely the **radical axis** of the original circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n   For two intersecting circles the radical axis is the line through their intersection points, i.e. \\(\\ell=AB\\).  \n   Consequently \\(\\ell\\) passes through \\(B\\) and is perpendicular to the line joining the centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\); thus  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell\\perp MN .\\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   In \\(\\triangle P'MN\\) the point \\(H'\\) is the orthocentre, so the altitude from the vertex \\(P'\\) is the line through \\(P'\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \n   By (2) this altitude coincides with the line through \\(P'\\) perpendicular to \\(\\ell\\); therefore the altitude from \\(P'\\) is exactly the line \\(H'P'\\).  \n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   H'P'\\perp \\ell ,\n   \\]\n\n   which is precisely (1).\n\n5. **Return to the original configuration.**  \n   The inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) is involutory; the statement “\\(H'P'\\perp \\ell\\)” is equivalent to “\\(HP\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)”.  \n   From step 1 we already know that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the line \\(HP\\).  \n   Therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }\\triangle BEF.}\n\\]\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch