qual the ratio of the opposite sides (the “symmedian criterion”).  \nEquation (8) is precisely that criterion for the vertex \\(A\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\); consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AP\\text{ is the }A\\text{-symmedian of }\\triangle BEF}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) equals the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nLet \\(O\\) be the centre of the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) (i.e. \\(O=A\\)).  \nBecause \\(\\mathcal I\\) interchanges the two circles, the image of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is the line \\(CD\\); conversely, the image of the line \\(CD\\) is that circumcircle.  \nSince the circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is the centre of that circle, it is fixed by the inversion:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(P)=P . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nApply \\(\\mathcal I\\) to the triangle \\(PMN\\).  \nPoints \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are the centres of the two circles, hence they are exchanged by the inversion:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(M)=N ,\\qquad \\mathcal I(N)=M . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nUsing (4)–(5) we see that the image of the side \\(PM\\) is the line through \\(P\\) and \\(F\\) (because \\(M\\) goes to \\(N\\) and the line \\(PM\\) is taken to the line through the images of its two points).  \nSimilarly, the image of \\(PN\\) is the line \\(PE\\).  \nConsequently the triangle \\(\\mathcal I(PMN)\\) is the triangle formed by the lines \\(PF\\) and \\(PE\\) together with the side \\(MN\\).\n\nNow recall the well‑known fact (proved by a simple angle chase) that the reflections of a vertex of a triangle in the two sides through that vertex lie on the circumcircle of the triangle and that the orthocenter is the common intersection of the three such reflections.  \nApplying this to \\(\\triangle BEF\\) we obtain:\n\n* the reflection of \\(B\\) in the side \\(BE\\) is the point \\(F\\);\n* the reflection of \\(B\\) in the side \\(BF\\) is the point \\(E\\).\n\nThus the 