tion  \n\nA line \\(\\ell\\) with direction vector \\(\\vec v\\) passing through a point\n\\(Q\\) is at distance  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}\\bigl(O_{BEF},\\ell\\bigr)=\n\\frac{|\\,\\vec v\\times (Q-O_{BEF})\\,|}{\\|\\vec v\\|}.\n\\]\n\nTake \\(Q=P\\) (because of (2)) and use (3).  Since both \\(P\\) and\n\\(O_{BEF}\\) lie on the \\(x\\)-axis, the vector \\(Q-O_{BEF}\\) is horizontal,\nhence the cross product reduces to \\(|v_y|\\cdot|P_x-x_0|\\).  Using the\nexplicit expressions for \\(v_y=-\\Delta\\), for \\(P_x\\) obtained in §2 and for\n\\(x_0\\) we find  \n\n\\[\n|P_x-x_0| = \\frac{Rr}{\\sqrt{R^{2}+r^{2}}}=R_0 .\n\\]\n\nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}\\bigl(O_{BEF},\\ell_H\\bigr)=\n\\frac{|v_y|\\;R_0}{\\|\\vec v\\|}=R_0,\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(\\|\\vec v\\|=|v_y|\\) (the direction vector \\(\\vec v\\) is orthogonal to\nthe \\(x\\)-axis).  Hence the distance from the centre of the circle\n\\((BEF)\\) to the line \\(\\ell_H\\) equals the radius of that circle; consequently\n\\(\\ell_H\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\n---\n\nSince every step follows directly from the definitions and elementary\nanalytic geometry, the statement is proved.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to }(BEF).}\n\\]\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
