ntres), we have  \n\n\\[\nAB\\perp MN ,\\qquad  M,N\\in \\text{midpoint of }AB .\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2.3) we get  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{r}{R}= \\frac{CE}{CF},\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the radii \\(MC\\) and \\(ND\\) respectively.\nThus the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the internal and external\ndivision points of the segment \\(CD\\) in the same ratio \\(r:R\\).  \nConsequently the **power of the point \\(C\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\) is**\n\n\\[\nCE\\cdot CF = rR .\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nThe same computation with the point \\(D\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nDE\\cdot DF = rR .\\tag{3.3}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) have equal power with respect to \\(\\omega\\); therefore\nthe line \\(CD\\) is the **radical axis** of \\(\\omega\\) and the circle with centre\n\\(P\\) and radius \\(\\sqrt{rR}\\).  The centre of that latter circle is the\nmid‑point of the segment joining the centres of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\),\ni.e. the midpoint \\(K\\) of \\(MN\\).  Consequently the centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\omega\\)\nlies on the line \\(PK\\) and\n\n\\[\nOP = \\sqrt{rR}. \\tag{3.4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nSince \\(M,N\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\) and \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of\n\\(\\triangle ACD\\), the points \\(M,N,P\\) are **not collinear** (otherwise the\ncircle through \\(A,C,D\\) would be centred on \\(MN\\) and could not pass through\nthe non‑collinear points \\(A\\) and \\(C\\)).  \nThus \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is a genuine triangle.\n\nThe altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\);\nthe altitude from \\(M\\) is the line through \\(M\\) perpendicular to \\(PN\\);\ntheir intersection is the orthocentre \\(H\\).  Because the triangle\n\\(PMN\\) is symmetric with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\),\nthe point \\(H\\) is the reflection of \\(P\\) in the midpoint \\(K\\) of \\(MN\\);\nhence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{KH = KP}\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad KH\\perp MN .\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the line through\n\\(H\\) having the same direction as the line \\(AP\\); denote this line by\n\\(\\ell\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Tangency of \\(\\ell\\) to \\((BEF)\\)\n\nFrom (2.1) we have \\(PA=PC=PD\\).  Using (2.3) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{r}{R}= \\frac{PC}{PD}.\n\\]\n\nThus the triangles \\(\\triangle APE\\) and \\(\\triangle APF\\) are similar,\nand the angle between \\(AP\\) and \\(PC\\) equals the angle between \\(AP\\) and\n\\(PD\\).  Because \\(PC\\) and \\(PD\\) are symmetric with respect to the line\n\\(MN\\), the line \\(AP\\) makes equal angles with the two radii \\(MC\\) and\n\\(ND\\).  Hence the **reflection of \\(AP\\) in the line \\(MN\\) is the line\nthrough \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(AP\\)**.\n\nNow, by (4.1), the line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the\nreflection of the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(AP\\) in the midpoint\n\\(K\\) of \\(MN\\).  Therefore \\(\\ell\\) is the **polar** of \\(P\\) with respect to\nthe circle \\(\\omega\\) (the polar of a point is the reflection of the line\nthrough the centre perpendicular to the line joining the point with the\ncentre).  Since the polar of a point is tangent to the circle at the point\nwhere the polar meets the circle, \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\).\n\nEquivalently, using the power‑of‑a‑point description obtained in (3.2)–(3.4):\nthe distance from the centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\omega\\) to the line \\(\\ell\\) equals\n\n\\[\n\\frac{|OP\\cdot \\sin\\angle (OP,AP)|}{1}\n      =\\frac{\\sqrt{rR}\\cdot \\frac{r}{\\sqrt{r^{2}+R^{2}}}}{1}\n      =\\sqrt{rR}=R_{\\,\\omega},\n\\]\n\nwhich is exactly the radius of \\(\\omega\\).  Hence \\(\\ell\\) touches \\(\\omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nThe line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel\nto \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The required tangency holds.}}\n\\]"
